# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Frontend environment variables.
#
# Local dev:  copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in the values.
# Vercel:     set each of these under Project -> Settings -> Environment
#             Variables (Production + Preview), then redeploy.
#
# Every var here is NEXT_PUBLIC_* — it is bundled into the browser build.
# That is fine: neither is a secret, just URLs.
#
# If ALL of these are absent the app still builds and runs in "demo mode"
# (sample data, auth disabled). Set them to switch to the real backend.
#
# No Supabase variables here anymore — authentication, session cookies and
# all data now go through this app's own backend (see ARCHITECTURE.md and
# SECURITY.md's "Authentication decision"). NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL/
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY are dead if still set from an older
# deployment; harmless (nothing in the app reads them), but safe to remove
# from Vercel's env var list.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Backend API root — the Hostinger VPS's own domain (see DEPLOYMENT.md),
# NOT the VPS's bare IP address: browser code must never reference an
# internal IP directly, both because it isn't stable across a future VPS
# migration and because TLS is only valid for the hostname.
# Do NOT include a trailing slash and do NOT append /api/v1 (the app adds it).
# Staging:    https://test.spidnums.com
# Production: https://api.spidnums.com (once that DNS record exists)
#
# If this is unset in production the app silently falls back to
# http://localhost:8000, which is dead there, and quietly shows demo data.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://test.spidnums.com

# This deployment's own public URL — used for canonical links / SEO metadata.
# For this project that is your Vercel production domain.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://speed-num.vercel.app
